# find - search drawer trees for files by name, size, date, kind

## SYNOPSIS
    find [path | pattern ...] [-name PAT] [-type f|d|l]
         [-size [+-]N[ckM]] [-mtime [+-]N] [-newer FILE] [-empty]
         [-maxdepth N] [-mindepth N] [! -o -a ( )]
         [-print] [-prune] [-delete] [-exec CMD {} ;]

## DESCRIPTION
Goes through every drawer below the paths given, all the way down, and
asks the *expression* (the tests after the paths) about every file and
drawer it meets. Those that pass are printed, one path a line, or handed
to an action such as **-exec** or **-delete**. It works as GNU find does.

Each drawer's entries are taken in natural order (`file9` before
`file10`). With no path, the current drawer is searched; the names are
then printed relative to it (`x`, `sub/x`). A path is printed as given,
with the names under it joined by `/` (or right after the `:` of a
volume: `RAM:x`).

Several tests in a row must all be true. Use **-o** for "or", **!** for
"not", and `(` `)` to group them.

## OPTIONS
Tests:
- **-name** PAT
  the name matches the Amiga pattern PAT; `\*` works as `#?`. Upper and
  lower case are always the same, as with Amiga names.
- **-iname** PAT
  the same as **-name**.
- **-type** T
  f a file, d a drawer, l a link (soft link or hard link). Several at
  once: `-type fl` or `-type f,l`.
- **-size** [+-]N[c|b|k|M|G]
  the size in those units, rounded up: plain N and c are bytes, b blocks
  of 512 bytes, k M G kilo, mega, giga (1024s). `+N` more than N, `-N`
  less than N. Because of the rounding up, `-size -1k` finds only empty
  files; use `-size -1024` for "under 1 KB".
- **-mtime** [+-]N
  changed N days ago, in whole 24 hours (0 is the last 24 hours).
  `-mtime -7` is the last week.
- **-mmin** [+-]N
  changed N minutes ago.
- **-newer** FILE
  changed after FILE was.
- **-empty** an empty file, or a drawer with nothing in it.
- **-true**, **-false** always true, never true.

Actions:
- **-print** print the path and a new line. This is what happens when
  the expression has no action in it.
- **-print0** print the path followed by a 0 byte (for `xargs -0`).
- **-prune** do not go into this drawer.
- **-delete** delete it. Turns on **-depth**, so the contents of a drawer
  go before the drawer. A volume or assign itself (`Work:`) and the
  current drawer are never deleted; a drawer that still has something in
  it is not deleted, as Delete would refuse it.
- **-exec** CMD ARGS ;
  run the command, with every `{}` replaced by the path. True when the
  command returns 0.
- **-exec** CMD {} +
  run the command once with many paths at a time.

Operators, strongest first: `(` EXPR `)`; `!` EXPR or **-not** EXPR;
EXPR EXPR or EXPR **-a** EXPR (**-and**); EXPR **-o** EXPR (**-or**).

Options for the whole search (they may stand anywhere):
- **-maxdepth** N
  go no deeper than N levels below each path (0: only the paths).
- **-mindepth** N
  leave out what is less than N levels down (1 leaves out the paths).
- **-depth** each drawer after its contents.

## EXAMPLES
    find Work: -name #?.c
    find SYS: -type f -size +100k
    find Work:Projects -mtime -1
    find RAM: -name #?.bak -delete
    find Work:Docs -name #?.txt -exec grep -l Amiga {} ";"
    find Work:Projects -name #?.o | xargs rm

## RETURN CODES
0 fine, also when nothing matched; 5 something could not be read or
deleted, or a command could not be run; 10 a bad expression; 20 stopped
with Ctrl-C.

## NOTES
The Shell takes an unquoted `;` as the start of a comment. After
**-exec** write `";"` or `\;`, or simply end the line there.

With `{} +`, quote the `+`: at the end of a line the Shell takes it for
"more lines follow". Quote `>` and `<` meant for the command too
(`">NIL:"`), or the Shell takes them for find itself. Words with spaces
are quoted for the command. Its input is the console, else NIL:.

Soft links, and hard links to drawers, are never gone into. find keeps
its list of drawers to visit in memory, not on the Shell's stack, so no
tree is too deep for it.

A disk that is not in its drive gives a message, not a requester.

`find ?` shows the usage line.

